pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% `pol 1 4 * 4 d` multiplies a 2^62 coefficient out of i64 and divides it back
% in. The live checker promotes to i128 for the intermediate and returns an
% i64-representable result; the proved kernel's NARROW domain refuses the
% multiply, because the intermediate leaves the machine word.
%
% This used to pin the reason that names exactly that pairing --
% `kernel-guard-refused-with-narrow-result`, a counted SKIP rather than a
% divergence, since a derivation may legitimately leave i64 and come back. It
% now pins the stronger fact: the bridge re-runs the refused step at the
% kernel's unbounded instantiation, where the intermediate is exact, and
% verifies the whole derivation. The distinction the old reason drew -- "dipped
% out and came back" versus "wrapped" -- no longer has to be left undecided,
% because the wide replay decides it (see the development design notes section 7.3).
f 1;
pol 1 4 * 4 d;
output NONE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
